TA-authid
====

Collects identity-client state: name-service lookup order from /etc/nsswitch.conf, SSSD service state and configured domains, system Kerberos ticket cache count, and nscd/oddjob presence where installed. Emits JSON events to index=main (sourcetype authid:status) and authid.* metrics to index=metrics (sourcetype authid:metrics); local-auth-only hosts report the baseline without alarms.

Prerequisites
-------------
  * none - reads /etc/nsswitch.conf, systemctl, klist, sssctl when present
  * Splunk Enterprise or Universal Forwarder 9.x - 10.6 (forwarder recommended
    on monitored hosts).
  * A metrics-typed index (default name: metrics) on the indexer:

      [metrics]
      datatype = metric

    Forwarders only need that index to exist on the indexer side.
  * No network egress, no root requirement beyond the Splunk service account.
    Fully standalone add-on with no dependencies.

Install
-------
Install on each monitored host (Apps > Install app from file, splunk install
app, or deployment server), restart Splunk, then verify:

  index=main sourcetype="authid:status" | head

Search quick reference
----------------------
  index=main sourcetype="authid:status" | head
  | mcatalog values(metric_name) where index=metrics metric_name="authid.*" by host
  | mstats avg("authid.*") where index=metrics span=60s by host

Usage
-----
Events: index=main sourcetype=authid:status  (nsswitch order, sssd state/domains, krb ticket count)
Metrics: authid.sssd_active, authid.krb_tickets, authid.nscd_active on index=metrics
Dashboards: TA-Authid > Authid Overview / Authid Metrics

Configure
---------
Override the interval or destination index in etc/apps/TA-authid/local/inputs.conf
(never edit default/).

Optional: SSSD domains
----------------------
/etc/sssd/sssd.conf is 0600 root:root, so the Splunk service account can
never read it directly and sssd_domains would always be empty. When the
config file is unreadable, the collector falls back to
`sudo -n /usr/sbin/sssctl domain-list` (installed separately; sssd-tools)
and parses its "Domain: <name>" lines. To enable that fallback, install the
supplied sudoers drop-in yourself - it is shipped as an example only and is
never installed by the app:

  sudo install -m 0440 <app>/deploy/ta_authid.sssctl_sudoers.example /etc/sudoers.d/ta-authid

(Replace the user "splunk" if your forwarder runs under another account.)
Without the drop-in - or where sudo/sssctl are not installed - the
collector degrades gracefully: events still emit and sssd_domains stays [].

Troubleshooting
---------------
  * klist reads the system ticket cache only when a keytab/agent is present.
  * krb_tickets counts tickets ("Valid until" lines from klist);
    krb_ticket_caches counts caches ("Ticket cache:" lines from klist -S).
  * SSSD sections appear only when sssd is installed; see "Optional: SSSD
    domains" above for reading domains without root-owned file access.

License
-------
SOURCE-AVAILABLE (see LICENSE) - free personal, educational and evaluation
use; commercial or production use requires a paid license from BentleyPC
LLC (https://www.bentleypc.com/TA-authid).

Enter your key (format BP-...) after purchase using EITHER method:

  1. Splunk Web: open the app, click "License" in the nav, click a row to
     open the key entry page, paste the key and Save.

  2. File (headless / forwarder friendly): create the file
     $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/apps/TA-authid/local/license.conf with:

         [settings]
         telemetry_enabled = 1
         key = BP-xxxxxxxxxxxx

     No restart required - the hourly beacon reads this file on every run.

Set telemetry_enabled = 0 to keep the key for validation but pause all
outbound beacons. The beacon sends only: license key, hostname, app name
and app version to the BentleyPC license API (see api_base in
default/license.conf). It is telemetry only - never a license check that
blocks functionality.
